 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on with Danon disease",
   "text": "Danon disease is caused by changes, called mutations, in a gene named LAMP2 [Eb39aff7, E91e8398]. In most people with Danon disease, these changes mean the body makes very little or none of the LAMP2 protein [E2638519]. The LAMP2 protein normally sits on lysosomes, which are tiny parts inside cells that act like clean-up and recycling stations [E6303c40]. Without enough LAMP2, cells struggle to clear away old or damaged parts, a clean-up process called autophagy [Ed8d56f4, E1018ef1]. This means leftover material builds up inside cells and gets in the way of how the cells normally work [E3dcae5a]. This buildup mostly affects the heart and muscles, and can lead to serious heart problems [E13cc433, Ef11c52b]. Some research also points to trouble in mitochondria, the tiny parts of cells that help make energy [E86ac16a].",
   "citations": [
